  (acetaminophen), alone or in combination with an antiemetic, compared with placebo and other active interventions in  
  the treatment of acute migraine in adults.", "background": "This is an updated version of the original Cochrane       
  review published in Issue 11, 2010 (Derry 2010). Migraine is a common, disabling condition and a burden for the       
  individual, health services and society. Many sufferers choose not to, or are unable to, seek professional help and   
  rely on over‐the‐counter analgesics. Co‐therapy with an antiemetic should help to reduce nausea and vomiting, which   
  are commonly associated with migraine.", "selection_criteria": "We included randomised, double‐blind, placebo‐ or     
